>
  <section class="case-reading mx-auto max-w-[820px] px-6 pt-12 sm:px-12">
    <p>La Borda brings 28 homes and shared rooms around a covered central patio in Barcelona. Completed in 2018, the resident cooperative uses publicly owned land through a 75-year lease. Its cession-of-use model gives members a right to occupy rather than an individually tradable apartment. <a class="source-link" href="https://lacol.coop/projectes/la-borda/">[Project source]</a></p>
    <p>Lacol describes the patio, shading and ventilation as parts of a passive environmental strategy. Shared spaces include cooking, laundry, guests and care, distributed through the building. These are design intentions and reported features—not measured proof that an atrium will cool a different building in a different climate.</p>
    <h2>The route home can be part of home.</h2>
    <p>The useful move is giving the everyday trip to the front door somewhere to pause. A bench near laundry may do more for a Tuesday than a magnificent room that is always locked. That is a design hypothesis worth testing with residents: who wants company, who needs quiet, and who can comfortably use the route?</p>
    <p>A covered court also has to work on the awkward days: cooking smells, noise, heat, smoke, a broken opening mechanism. Ask for a maintenance plan and seasonal modeling before calling it a climate solution.</p>
    <aside class="research-note mt-8">Reading lens: project publications document the place and its intended design. They are not independent evaluations of affordability, energy performance or how every resident experiences life there. The questions here are our interpretation.</aside>
  </section>

